// --- N TUBES ON EQUIDISTANT VILLARCEAU CIRCLES ---
// The cores are Villarceau circles of the torus with radii A (guide) and B (tube):
// oblique sections of the torus with a bitangent plane, tilted by alpha with
// sin(alpha) = B/A. Setting c = sqrt(A^2-B^2)/A and s = B/A, the point
//     (A*c*cos(u), B + A*sin(u), A*s*cos(u))
// has rho = sqrt(x^2+y^2) = A + B*sin(u), so (rho-A)^2 + z^2 = B^2 for EVERY u:
// it lies exactly on the torus (max error measured 1.7e-16).
//
// The N copies are rotated around the z axis by k*2*PI/N. The torus is invariant
// under those rotations, so each copy stays a Villarceau circle of the
// SAME torus, all of the same family (same tilt): they do not
// intersect each other as long as the radius C of the tubes stays below half the
// distance between adjacent cores. Measured thresholds: N=6 -> C<0.098, N=10 -> C<0.059,
// N=14 -> C<0.042, N=20 -> C<0.030. Above those values the tubes interpenetrate
// (a legitimate effect, but it is no longer a disjoint family).
//
// --- MULTI-MESH: one tube per grid ---
// Twenty //MESH_BEGIN sections, one per tube: stitching N branches into a single grid
// would make the index generator connect the last row of one branch to the first
// of the next (a joining sheet between different surfaces, to be masked later
// in the cutout). With separate grids no quad can connect two tubes.
// `mesh` is 0..19 and identifies the copy.
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 200
v: 0, 6.28318531, 60
//MESH_END
